description:
metadata.rs:258-272 — the unit test `annotation_deserializes_from_spec_example` uses:
```rust
match meta {
    jmap_metadata_types::Metadata::Annotation(a) => {
        assert_eq!(a.related_type, "Email");
        // ... 3 more assertions ...
    }
    _ => panic!("expected Annotation variant"),
}
```

A let-else binding expresses the same intent with one less level of indentation and no wildcard arm:
```rust
let jmap_metadata_types::Metadata::Annotation(a) = meta
    else { panic!("expected Annotation variant"); };
assert_eq!(a.related_type, "Email");
// ... 3 more assertions ...
```

What goes wrong if it stays: nothing — both compile, both produce identical test behaviour. The let-else form (Rust 1.65+, fine under workspace edition 2024) is a minor readability gain. Labelled `opinion` because the engineering benefit is marginal.

Scope: metadata.rs:258-272 (one test site). The pattern is metadata-client-specific — canonical mail-client tests don't have this exact pattern.
